window.SEARCH_INDEX = [{"title":"AI Safety at Work | Occupational Health and Prevention","description":"An independent approach to AI safety grounded in real work: understand, assess and govern how AI deployment affects occupational health.","keywords":"AI · WORK · PREVENTION AI safety is also an occupational health issue. Independent guidance for understanding how AI systems transform work, and how to anticipate their effects on health. Assess a deployment → Discover our approach 01 — THE PROBLEM We have expanded AI capabilities. Not yet our definition of its safety. AI safety brings together research and practices intended to make AI systems reliable, controllable and safe, from design through use. Yet the debate still focuses mainly on risks within the models themselves — errors, bias, cybersecurity or misuse — and far less on what happens when they enter an organisation. At work, AI changes tasks, deadlines, room for judgement, responsibilities and skills. Its effects therefore depend not only on technical performance, but on how it is deployed, controlled and discussed. Broadening AI safety means examining these transformations before deployment and then in real activity: cognitive load, autonomy, surveillance, work quality, collective work and health. This perspective concerns researchers, employers, worker representatives, public authorities and occupational safety and health professionals together. FOR YOU See how this question concerns you. I am an employee an employer or executive an HR professional an occupational physician an occupational safety and health professional a worker representative an AI designer or engineer a researcher a public decision-maker Assess a project → A WARNING Thomas Cole The Consummation of Empire , 1836 In Cole’s series, this city at the height of its power immediately precedes chaos. It reminds us how quickly an equilibrium that seems secure can become fragile and tip if we stop paying attention. View the work ↗ 02 — THE ARGUMENT A model never acts alone. The health effects of an AI system do not come from the model alone. They emerge from the way its capabilities are integrated into the organisation of work. 01 Model Capabilities Errors Data 02 Work system Targets Deadlines Control · Staffing 03 Real activity Workload Autonomy Skills 04 Health Physical risks Psychosocial risks 03 — ACT Three ways to act. 01 UNDERSTAND What these systems can actually do. An introduction to model capabilities, limits and errors. Explore → 02 ASSESS What changes when AI enters real work. Prepare a deployment and monitor its effects on activity and health. Assess → 03 GOVERN Decide how AI may — or may not — transform work. Guidance for organising a documented, reversible and monitored decision. View guidance → 04 — RESEARCH Recent work SCIENTIFIC ARTICLE / 2025 Large language models a","url":"en/"},{"title":"Dr Charles Broutin — AI, Occupational Health and Real Work","description":"Background, publications and activities of Dr Charles Broutin, occupational physician and AI lead for the French Society of Occupational Health.","keywords":"About · Occupational medicine · Artificial intelligence Charles Broutin, occupational physician working at the intersection of AI and real work. As an occupational physician, AI lead for the French Society of Occupational Health, author and trainer, I develop resources to examine professional AI uses and their effects on health. Explore my background ↓ View publications Clinical practice Collective prevention Independent resource Professional profile Paris · France Dr Charles Broutin Occupational physician · AI lead for the French Society of Occupational Health Role SPSTN · Training & Methods Unit Contact LinkedIn profile ↗ Physician Occupational health Practice grounded in contact with workers, occupations and organisations. SFST Artificial intelligence lead Monitoring and knowledge-sharing for occupational health professionals. SPSTN Training & methods Head of the unit devoted to tools, practices and training. Independent Editorial project Dated, sourced content that is corrected as evidence develops. 01 / Background TRAINING Occupational medicine · University of Lille Clinical and preventive training focused on the links between health, activity and organisation. 2023 University diploma in artificial intelligence in health Additional training to understand models, their uses and limits in health. 2025 Medical residency thesis · Bossons Futé Prize Research on using large language models to identify wording defects in occupational physician recommendations. TODAY Physician, trainer, author and AI lead Research, monitoring and knowledge-sharing devoted to AI uses at work. 02 / Publications Research connecting language models, work quality and prevention. Two publications addressing different questions: the psychosocial effects of introducing LLMs and their use as a second reader of medical wording. 2025 Psychosocial risks Large language models and emerging psychosocial issues at work. The article proposes examining the introduction of LLMs through the six factors in the Gollac report: intensity, emotional demands, autonomy, social relationships, value conflicts and insecurity. Archives des Maladies Professionnelles et de l’Environnement , 86, 102877. Open the DOI ↗ 2026 Quality of recommendations Artificial intelligence to improve the precision of occupational physician recommendations. The study evaluates a language model as a second reader for identifying several categories of defects, without replacing work-post analysis or the occupational physician’s decision. Références en Santé au Travail , TF 335, no. 186, June 2026. Read the article on the INRS ","url":"en/about/"},{"title":"AI Safety and AGI | Scaling Laws, Benchmarks and Risks","description":"Understand AGI, scaling laws, frontier-model capabilities, AI benchmarks and safety risks, from model evaluation to deployment at work and in society.","keywords":"Technology frontier · Critical reference points AI safety & AGI. Models are advancing rapidly, but “general intelligence”, benchmark performance, autonomy and reliability are not the same thing. This page explains what is changing — and why safety must advance at the same pace. Start with AGI ↓ View benchmarks Primary sources Maintained links Verified 8 August 2026 When control gives way Thomas Cole Destruction · 1836 The city appears powerful until the balances supporting it give way. The work reminds us that performance and apparent control are not enough: without vigilance, room to step back and the ability to stop, a system can quickly become fragile and tip. View the artwork ↗ 01 AGI has no universal definition. Several thresholds have been proposed, but no single recognised test can declare that a system is AGI. 02 Scaling laws are observations. They describe measured trends when data, model size and compute increase — not a law guaranteeing intelligence. 03 A high score remains task-specific. Each benchmark measures particular tasks under particular conditions. It summarises neither reliability, real-world utility nor safety. 04 Safety must precede autonomy. The more a system can act, the more explicit its access, controls, stopping mechanisms, monitoring and accountability must be. The missing bridge Model safety is not enough when a system transforms work. Model-level risks, real deployment conditions and their effects on health are distinct but inseparable levels of analysis. This connection defines the site’s approach to AI safety at work and occupational health and its framework for AI-related psychosocial risks . 01 Model safety Reliability, robustness, misuse → 02 Deployment safety Use, access, control, accountability → 03 Work system Tasks, workload, autonomy → 04 Occupational health Prevent and monitor effects 01 / Define without oversimplifying What is artificial general intelligence? AGI is a research and foresight concept, not a settled technical category. Definitions differ over the human reference level, breadth of tasks and expected degree of autonomy. An economic definition Outperform humans in most economically valuable activities. OpenAI’s Charter defines AGI as highly autonomous systems that outperform humans at most economically valuable work. This definition emphasises broad utility and autonomy. Read the OpenAI Charter ↗ A classification by levels Measure breadth of capabilities, performance level and autonomy separately. Google DeepMind researchers propose classifying systems by the range of tasks they can perform and their ","url":"en/ai-safety-agi/"},{"title":"Workplace AI Assessment | Before and After Deployment","description":"Methodological presentation of an exploratory questionnaire designed to assess the psychosocial effects of a workplace AI system before and after deployment.","keywords":"Assess · Instrument in development Where is your project now? Choose the path that matches the project stage. The detailed method and its limitations follow. Key point. These tools structure work analysis and collective discussion. They provide no diagnosis, clinical threshold or automatic evidence of compliance. Choose a path ↓ Read the method Before and after Nine dimensions No global score An equilibrium to follow Thomas Cole The Voyage of Life: Youth · 1842 The luminous palace makes the destination appear secure, while the boat remains exposed throughout the journey. The work reminds us that an expected benefit can quickly become fragile if the real conditions of deployment are not followed: workload, autonomy, skills, collective work and health. View the artwork ↗ 01 / Choose Three paths for three situations. Start with the project stage. Methodological limits remain visible in every path. Before deployment 18 questions · ≈ 10 min Identify anticipated effects. Describe the task, affected groups and planned conditions before beginning the pilot. No names requested Data processed locally Descriptive profile without a global score Start → After deployment Real work Compare observed effects. Review workload, autonomy, errors, skills and work groups after the system goes live. Same scope as the initial assessment Incidents and workarounds included Corrective decisions documented Plan follow-up → Research and depth 27 items · 9 dimensions Understand the long instrument. Review the T0/T1 architecture, provisional interpretation rules, limitations and validation programme. Three items per dimension No decision threshold Scientific validation still required Read the method ↓ 27 Contextualised items Three items per dimension to cover its content more fully. 9 Dimensions analysed Six from Gollac and three AI-related mechanisms. T0 / T1 Two parallel forms Anticipated effects before, observed effects after. 0 Permitted total score Each dimension and each incident must remain visible. 02 / Architecture The Gollac framework, supplemented by three AI-specific mechanisms. The instrument does not measure general concern about technology. Its questions describe concrete changes in workload, decisions, relationships and know-how. 18 items · Reference framework Six psychosocial risk factors. The six families in the Gollac report are applied to work changes caused or amplified by an AI system. G01–G03 Work intensity and working time G04–G06 Emotional demands G07–G09 Autonomy and room for manoeuvre G10–G12 Social relationships and recognition G13–G15 Value conflicts G16–G","url":"en/evaluate/"},{"title":"Assess an AI Project Before Deployment — Short Questionnaire","description":"An exploratory 18-item questionnaire for identifying potential psychosocial effects before a workplace AI project is deployed.","keywords":"Before deployment · Exploratory short form Assess an AI project before deployment. A guided 18-item pathway for examining the project through the six factors in the Gollac report and three AI-related dimensions. Start the assessment ↓ Understand the method Exploratory result No overall score At the end of the questionnaire A descriptive profile, not a verdict. The nine dimensions remain separate so that the mechanisms requiring discussion before the pilot remain visible. Output 01 Nine descriptive indicators Output 02 High-scoring items to examine Output 03 Discussion prompts Results remain on this page. No person, company or provider name is requested. 18 Questions Two items for each of the nine dimensions. ≈ 10 min Indicative duration Answer based on how the system is actually expected to operate. Local No data sent Answers do not leave the browser. Exploratory Not psychometric No diagnosis, certification or scientific threshold. 01 · Prepare Essential guidance 02 · Describe Context and use 03 · Assess One question at a time 04 · Interpret Profile and discussion points Initial assessment No identifying data Answer for the use that is actually planned. Use the most exposed population and the most demanding reasonably foreseeable situation. Do not rely only on the provider’s demonstration. 01 A specific use Choose a clearly defined task, population and organisation. 02 Missing information remains visible Choose “NI” rather than a middle response when the evidence is insufficient. 03 A collective discussion Then compare the result with the views of the people concerned. Tool status This short form is empirical and has not been scientifically validated. It is not a diagnosis, proof of compliance or authorisation to deploy. Describe the project → Step 02 Non-identifying context Describe the project without naming it. This information provides context for the profile. Do not enter any company, person, software or provider name. Main sector Choose a category Health, social care or prevention Industry, logistics or construction Services, consulting or support functions Government or public service Retail, hospitality or customer service Education, research or training Other sector Approximate organisation size Choose a range 1 to 10 workers 11 to 49 50 to 249 250 to 999 1,000 or more Project stage Choose a stage Project definition or solution selection Pilot in preparation Pilot recently started Deployment underway Population directly concerned Choose a range 1 to 10 people 11 to 50 51 to 250 More than 250 Assessment date This date will appear in the report. P","url":"en/evaluate/impact/"},{"title":"Workplace AI After Deployment | Follow-Up Questionnaire","description":"An 18-question post-deployment workplace AI follow-up covering workload, autonomy, skills, collective work, human oversight and observed psychosocial effects.","keywords":"After deployment · dated follow-up Monitor the effects of AI in real work Choose a defined observation period and document effects on workload, autonomy, skills, collective work, service quality and human oversight. Start follow-up Return to pre-deployment assessment 10 min indicative duration 18 questions Dated longitudinal comparison Anonymous local project code Use the same anonymous code at each follow-up to compare reports without identifying the organisation. i Exploratory, non-psychometric tool. The result supports observation and corrective action; it is not a diagnosis or proof of compliance. Pathway Dated follow-up 01 Prepare Observation period 02 Document Dates and uses 03 Observe Real-world effects 04 Correct Priorities and follow-up Before deployment? Open initial assessment → Guided questionnaire Post-deployment follow-up ● No identifying data requested Before you begin Define the period being observed Answer from real operation, including workarounds, team differences and uses that were not originally planned. 01 Stable anonymous code Use the same code for every follow-up. 02 Defined period Archive the PDF with previous reports. Describe the follow-up period Return to initial assessment Step 1 Timeline and context Dates and an anonymous code allow longitudinal comparison without identifying the project. Anonymous code: do not use the organisation, site, product or provider name. Anonymous project code Generate 4–16 letters, numbers or hyphens. Baseline assessment date Follow-up date Follow-up number Choose First follow-up Second follow-up Third follow-up Fourth or later Main sector Choose Health, social care or prevention Industry, logistics or construction Services, consulting or support functions Public administration or public service Retail, hospitality or customer service Education, research or training Other sector Organisation size Choose 1–10 workers 11–49 50–249 250–999 1,000 or more Population directly affected Choose 1–10 people 11–50 51–250 More than 250 Current deployment status Choose Limited pilot Progressive rollout Generalised deployment Paused or restricted Change since the last review Choose No major change Settings or rules changed Population or scope expanded Model, provider or data changed Organisation or objectives changed Several important changes AI uses being followed Select all that apply Generative assistant Drafting, summarising, searching or documentation Work organisation Planning, prioritisation or task allocation HR decisions Recruitment, evaluation, mobility or remuneration Monitoring or control Productivi","url":"en/evaluate/impact/follow-up.html"},{"title":"Workplace AI Law and Governance","description":"Qualify a workplace AI use, identify the applicable legal frameworks and organise a documented, reversible and monitored decision.","keywords":"Law & governance · From use to decision Govern AI through what it actually changes. Describe the use, identify applicable rules, involve the right people, and decide on a documented, reversible and monitored pilot. The starting point is not the tool’s name. Duties depend on the task being changed, the people concerned, the data used and the possible consequences of a system output. Qualify a use ↓ See my role Dated official sources Four distinct frameworks No automatic green light What holds the whole together Thomas Cole The Architect’s Dream · 1840 These architectures seem to form a stable whole, yet their equilibrium depends on how each part fits with the others. The work reminds us that governance holds only through clear responsibilities, effective routes for challenge and sustained attention to the system’s real effects. View the artwork ↗ 1 use Context-specific qualification The same technology may fall under different rules depending on the task and its effects. 4 Frameworks to examine The AI Act, GDPR, labour law and prevention duties may all apply. 5 Decision gates From a demonstrated need to real-world monitoring, with the ability to stop. 0 Universal approval No single document is sufficient to authorise every use of a system. 01 / Qualify the use Describe the system’s practical power before looking for the rule. Qualification starts neither from the provider’s brand nor from the word “assistance”. It starts from the activity, the data and the actual reach of the output. 01 Which task is being changed? Write, summarise, recommend, classify, recruit, assign a task, monitor or contribute to a decision. 02 Who is concerned? Applicants, workers, professionals, patients or service users — and the data used to describe or evaluate them. 03 What reach does the output have? A revisable draft, priority order, score, activity monitoring or a decision producing a significant effect. 04 How can human control be recovered? The reviewer’s competence and authority, ability to challenge, fallback solution and suspension conditions. 02 / Identify the frameworks Four sets of rules answering different questions. Examining them separately avoids two errors: believing a system that is “not high-risk” presents no issue, or asking the DPO alone to decide the whole project. 01 Progressive application EU Artificial Intelligence Act Qualify the system, use, organisation’s role and corresponding duties. Starting question Is the use prohibited, subject to a transparency duty or classified as high-risk? For a high-risk system used at work, the deployer must in particular","url":"en/legal-governance/"},{"title":"Legal notice","description":"Publisher, hosting, responsibility and intellectual property.","keywords":"Legal notice Publisher, responsibility and intellectual property. Publisher Personal website edited by Dr Charles Broutin , occupational physician. Publication director: Dr Charles Broutin. Editorial contact: santetravailia@gmail.com or professional LinkedIn profile . Purpose and responsibility The site provides educational and professional information. It does not replace individual medical care, an occupational-health opinion based on a real situation or case-specific legal advice. Intellectual property Original texts and site design remain protected. Thomas Cole paintings are public-domain works reproduced from Wikimedia Commons. External sources retain their respective rights. Editorial independence The site is an independent personal initiative. Mentioned institutions, publications and professional activities do not imply sponsorship or formal endorsement. Corrections Material errors or outdated links may be reported to the editorial contact. Corrections and major updates are dated. French legal notice The French version is the reference legal notice for the French website.","url":"en/legal-notice/"},{"title":"Privacy","description":"Technical data, local search, external links and visitor rights on the AI & Occupational Health website.","keywords":"Privacy policy Technical data, local search, external links and visitor rights. No account or form This version of the website provides no user account, contact form or proprietary newsletter subscription. The website does not automatically collect email addresses. Local search Internal search runs in your browser. Search terms are not sent to the editor or to a third-party search service. Audience measurement with Plausible The website uses Plausible Analytics to measure page views, traffic sources and, for selected links, clicks to the newsletter. Plausible may produce aggregate statistics about country or region, browser, operating system and device type. The purpose is to understand overall use and improve content, without advertising or individual profiling. Plausible uses no cookies, local storage or persistent identifier. The IP address and user agent are used transiently to create a daily identifier; they are not stored and the calculation salt is deleted every 24 hours. Visitor data is processed in the European Union. Read Plausible’s data policy ↗ Analytics retention Retention depends on the Plausible plan in use: the provider currently lists three years for Starter and Growth, five years for Business, and a contractual period for Enterprise. The editor controls the account and may delete the site statistics at any time. This information is checked whenever this policy is revised. Local questionnaire data The assessment tools run locally and may retain up to 50 results in your browser to show an aggregate local history. No identifying data is requested and no collective transmission is active while no collection endpoint is configured. You can erase these records by clearing this website’s local data in your browser settings. Technical hosting The website may be hosted by GitHub Pages. Like any web host, GitHub may process technical data required to secure and deliver the service, including connection logs, under its own privacy policy. External links and services Opening a publication, LinkedIn, Substack or another external website is governed by that service’s privacy policy. Before any visitor action, the browser currently loads Google Fonts and the Plausible measurement script described above. Contact and data minimisation Contact is voluntary through LinkedIn . Do not send health data, confidential company information or sensitive personal data. Your rights Where information is sent directly to the editor, you may request access, correction or deletion. You may also contact the French data protection authority, CNIL. Update Last revised: 1","url":"en/privacy/"},{"title":"Research — AI, work and occupational health","description":"Research, notes and observations on AI, work organisation, worker health and safe deployment.","keywords":"RESEARCH How does AI change work? Notes, research and observations on artificial intelligence, work organisation and occupational health. How should we make AI-driven work safe for humans? Featured Research All Research 01 Featured Research Selected work connecting technical capability, work organisation and prevention. NOTE AI & Work · Worker Health 16 AUG 2026 When AI expands the job before it replaces it AI lets workers perform tasks historically associated with other professions. This apparent autonomy may also shift workload, responsibility and verification work. READ NOTE → FIELD NOTE Algorithmic Management · Worker Health 15 AUG 2026 What happens when the manager is an AI? When a system assigns tasks, measures activity or recommends a decision, it changes authority without necessarily making responsibility clearer. READ NOTE → Thomas Cole · The Oxbow · 1836 RESEARCH AI Safety · Worker Health 14 AUG 2026 Occupational health is an AI safety issue A model can be technically reliable and still make work more intense, less autonomous or more uncertain. Safety must include the effects of the deployed system on people. READ NOTE → POLICY NOTE Governance & Prevention · AI Safety 13 AUG 2026 Compliance does not make AI deployment safe Legal classification creates obligations. It does not replace analysis of real work, discussion of use or monitoring after deployment. READ NOTE → 02 / ALL RESEARCH Research An evolving index of scientific publications, notes, field analysis and governance perspectives. (09) All AI & Work Worker Health AI Safety Algorithmic Management Governance & Prevention 9 publications shown 01 NOTE AI & Work Worker Health When AI expands the job before it replaces it AI lets workers perform tasks historically associated with other professions. This apparent autonomy may also shift workload, responsibility and verification work. 16 AUG 2026 READ → 02 FIELD NOTE Algorithmic Management Worker Health What happens when the manager is an AI? When a system assigns tasks, measures activity or recommends a decision, it changes authority without necessarily making responsibility clearer. 15 AUG 2026 READ → 03 RESEARCH AI Safety Worker Health Occupational health is an AI safety issue A model can be technically reliable and still make work more intense, less autonomous or more uncertain. Safety must include the effects of the deployed system on people. 14 AUG 2026 READ → 04 POLICY NOTE Governance & Prevention AI Safety Compliance does not make AI deployment safe Legal classification creates obligations. It does not replace analysis of real work, dis","url":"en/research/"},{"title":"AI changes work before it changes employment — Research","description":"Transformation begins with meaning, recognition, autonomy and the value of expertise — long before a job disappears.","keywords":"← All research NOTE · 05 AI & Work · Worker Health 12 AUG 2026 AI changes work before it changes employment Transformation begins with meaning, recognition, autonomy and the value of expertise — long before a job disappears. Author Dr Charles Broutin Published 12 AUG 2026 In this note 01 · The future is already unevenly distributed 02 · Make time for appropriation The future is already unevenly distributed When AI approaches the heart of a profession — searching, creating, judging or being recognised for expertise — its occupational effect cannot be reduced to productivity. It affects identity, meaning and each person’s place in the collective. Obsolescence anxiety, loss of recognition, dispossession, value conflicts and reduced autonomy can appear before any job is removed. Make time for appropriation Technology can change an activity faster than workers and collectives can rebuild its rules. Organisations must create time for discussion, preserve know-how and recognise new forms of contribution. A durable transformation does not merely ask people to keep pace with the model. It evolves targets, roles and quality criteria with them. 03 Why it matters Waiting for employment statistics means overlooking occupational and health transformations already under way. 04 Source LinkedIn — Charles Broutin Open the source ↗ Originally published on LinkedIn ↗ 05 Related notes 01 · NOTE When AI expands the job before it replaces it → 02 · FIELD NOTE The hidden work created by AI → 03 · FIELD NOTE What happens when the manager is an AI? →","url":"en/research/ai-changes-work-before-employment/"},{"title":"When AI expands the job before it replaces it — Research","description":"AI lets workers perform tasks historically associated with other professions. This apparent autonomy may also shift workload, responsibility and verification work.","keywords":"← All research NOTE · 01 AI & Work · Worker Health 16 AUG 2026 When AI expands the job before it replaces it AI lets workers perform tasks historically associated with other professions. This apparent autonomy may also shift workload, responsibility and verification work. Author Dr Charles Broutin Published 16 AUG 2026 In this note 01 · Job boundaries are becoming porous 02 · Observe the work that is added Job boundaries are becoming porous An OpenAI report indicates that 43.5% of work-related ChatGPT messages concerning a specific task involve an activity historically associated with another occupation. A salesperson can explore a dataset; a marketing professional can troubleshoot a website or write a simple script. This may widen autonomy and shorten delays. It may also add duties without removing existing workload, move verification to people who lack time or specialist expertise, and blur accountability. Observe the work that is added From an occupational-health perspective, the issue is not limited to job substitution. It also concerns work intensity, real autonomy, social relations, value conflicts and insecurity around changing skill expectations. A safe organisation makes this new work visible: verification, arbitration, recovery after error, learning and coordination with specialists. It does not confuse technical capability with the professional capacity to answer for consequences. 03 Why it matters The silent expansion of a job can precede visible automation by years. Prevention must examine what AI adds, removes and shifts in real work. 04 Source LinkedIn — Charles Broutin Open the source ↗ Originally published on LinkedIn ↗ 05 Related notes 01 · NOTE AI changes work before it changes employment → 02 · FIELD NOTE The hidden work created by AI → 03 · FIELD NOTE What happens when the manager is an AI? →","url":"en/research/ai-expands-the-job/"},{"title":"What happens when the manager is an AI? — Research","description":"When a system assigns tasks, measures activity or recommends a decision, it changes authority without necessarily making responsibility clearer.","keywords":"← All research FIELD NOTE · 02 Algorithmic Management · Worker Health 15 AUG 2026 What happens when the manager is an AI? When a system assigns tasks, measures activity or recommends a decision, it changes authority without necessarily making responsibility clearer. Author Dr Charles Broutin Published 15 AUG 2026 In this note 01 · Distributed authority 02 · Preserve the capacity to discuss Distributed authority Algorithmic management does not always replace a manager with an identifiable machine. It often distributes authority across a model, an interface, configured targets, historical data and the people expected to apply or challenge a recommendation. This can speed up coordination. It can also make it difficult to identify who actually sets the norm, explains a decision or owns an error. Preserve the capacity to discuss An assisted decision must remain understandable to those affected. They need to know which data mattered, who can correct the system and how to raise a situation the model does not represent. The prevention question is not whether the algorithm should be called a manager, but whether the organisation retains an accessible, competent and accountable human authority. 03 Why it matters When authority becomes diffuse, challenge and repair can disappear even as control over work increases. 04 Source IA Santé Travail — note de terrain Open the source ↗ 05 Related notes 01 · NOTE When AI expands the job before it replaces it → 02 · RESEARCH Occupational health is an AI safety issue → 03 · NOTE AI changes work before it changes employment →","url":"en/research/ai-managers-work/"},{"title":"Compliance does not make AI deployment safe — Research","description":"Legal classification creates obligations. It does not replace analysis of real work, discussion of use or monitoring after deployment.","keywords":"← All research POLICY NOTE · 04 Governance & Prevention · AI Safety 13 AUG 2026 Compliance does not make AI deployment safe Legal classification creates obligations. It does not replace analysis of real work, discussion of use or monitoring after deployment. Author Dr Charles Broutin Published 13 AUG 2026 In this note 01 · Law sets a floor 02 · Prevention examines the situation Law sets a floor Compliance helps identify necessary responsibilities, documentation and controls. It provides a common language for handling data, informing people and governing certain high-risk systems. Yet a compliant project can still be poorly fitted to the task, damage work quality or quietly move responsibility onto the user. Prevention examines the situation Safety requires testing rules against work as actually performed: room for manoeuvre, exceptions, deadlines, control capacity, skills and effects on collectives. Law addresses what is permitted and required. Prevention adds another question: under these precise conditions, does this deployment protect people over time? 03 Why it matters Confusing compliance with safety creates a blind spot precisely where organisational effects emerge. 04 Source IA Santé Travail — analyse de gouvernance Open the source ↗ 05 Related notes 01 · POLICY NOTE Who is responsible for safe AI deployment at work? → 02 · RESEARCH Occupational health is an AI safety issue → 03 · POLICY NOTE France is beginning to treat AI as an occupational-health issue →","url":"en/research/compliance-does-not-make-deployment-safe/"},{"title":"France is beginning to treat AI as an occupational-health issue —…","description":"France’s 2026–2030 occupational-health plan places AI-related transformations within prevention and calls for a national observatory on AI at work.","keywords":"← All research POLICY NOTE · 09 Governance & Prevention · Worker Health 08 AUG 2026 France is beginning to treat AI as an occupational-health issue France’s 2026–2030 occupational-health plan places AI-related transformations within prevention and calls for a national observatory on AI at work. Author Dr Charles Broutin Published 08 AUG 2026 In this note 01 · An explicit object of prevention 02 · Move from principle to observation An explicit object of prevention France’s 2026–2030 occupational-health plan asks that AI-related transformations be discussed, respectful of working conditions and evaluated through their real effects. It treats AI both as a prevention tool and as a possible source of occupational risk. This institutional shift matters: it brings AI use into ordinary questions of organisation, social dialogue and health instead of leaving it solely to technical teams. Move from principle to observation The planned observatory can produce useful knowledge if its data remain connected to work situations, occupations and concrete deployment choices. Occupational-health and prevention services have a role here: documenting both sides of AI, supporting discussion and monitoring effects over time. 03 Why it matters Recognising AI as a prevention issue makes it possible to act before organisational effects become invisible or normalised. 04 Source Plan santé au travail 2026–2030 Open the source ↗ 05 Related notes 01 · NOTE When AI expands the job before it replaces it → 02 · FIELD NOTE What happens when the manager is an AI? → 03 · RESEARCH Occupational health is an AI safety issue →","url":"en/research/france-ai-occupational-health/"},{"title":"The hidden work created by AI — Research","description":"Checking, reframing, integrating, reporting and recovering from error: automation removes some operations while creating others that often go uncounted.","keywords":"← All research FIELD NOTE · 06 Worker Health · AI & Work 11 AUG 2026 The hidden work created by AI Checking, reframing, integrating, reporting and recovering from error: automation removes some operations while creating others that often go uncounted. Author Dr Charles Broutin Published 11 AUG 2026 In this note 01 · Production accelerates; control remains 02 · Measure the complete chain Production accelerates; control remains A generative system produces an answer quickly, but someone must still verify its accuracy, legality and fitness. This vigilance is work: it requires time, skills, attention and sufficient authority to reject the output. When that work is not recognised, the tool’s visible gain may be purchased through silent intensification. Measure the complete chain Evaluation should include data preparation, prompting, verification, correction, documentation and recovery after incidents. The relevant indicator is not generation time alone. It is the total time needed to produce an outcome for which a person can genuinely take responsibility. 03 Why it matters An incomplete productivity gain can become shifted, invisible workload. Work does not disappear because it is unmeasured. 04 Source IA Santé Travail — observation organisationnelle Open the source ↗ 05 Related notes 01 · NOTE When AI expands the job before it replaces it → 02 · NOTE AI changes work before it changes employment → 03 · FIELD NOTE What happens when the manager is an AI? →","url":"en/research/hidden-work-created-by-ai/"},{"title":"AI can improve productivity. Managers may not know where. — Research","description":"Only 7.8% of managers in the study correctly anticipate differing effects across tasks. Heterogeneous performance can then become a homogeneous expectation.","keywords":"← All research RESEARCH · 08 AI & Work · Algorithmic Management 09 AUG 2026 AI can improve productivity. Managers may not know where. Only 7.8% of managers in the study correctly anticipate differing effects across tasks. Heterogeneous performance can then become a homogeneous expectation. Author Dr Charles Broutin Published 09 AUG 2026 In this note 01 · An average becomes a norm 02 · Change the unit of analysis An average becomes a norm The study distinguishes tasks on which AI markedly improves performance from others on which it degrades it. Yet only 7.8% of managers correctly anticipate this distinction, while 92.2% expect improvement across all tasks. Organisational risk emerges when a heterogeneous technological effect becomes a homogeneous expectation: raised targets, shortened deadlines or mandatory use regardless of task and person. Change the unit of analysis Benefit does not depend only on task, model and worker. It also depends on organisation and deployment choices: training, permission to doubt, verification, workload and the capacity to report failure. Better deployment means not only adapting workers to the tool, but adapting the organisation and use of the tool to real work. 03 Why it matters If benefits are wrongly generalised, the organisation may try to correct the worker when the problem lies in the task, model or deployment. 04 Source LinkedIn — analyse d’une étude organisationnelle Open the source ↗ Originally published on LinkedIn ↗ 05 Related notes 01 · NOTE When AI expands the job before it replaces it → 02 · FIELD NOTE What happens when the manager is an AI? → 03 · NOTE AI changes work before it changes employment →","url":"en/research/managers-misread-ai-productivity/"},{"title":"Occupational health is an AI safety issue — Research","description":"A model can be technically reliable and still make work more intense, less autonomous or more uncertain. Safety must include the effects of the deployed system on people.","keywords":"← All research RESEARCH · 03 AI Safety · Worker Health 14 AUG 2026 Occupational health is an AI safety issue A model can be technically reliable and still make work more intense, less autonomous or more uncertain. Safety must include the effects of the deployed system on people. Author Dr Charles Broutin Published 14 AUG 2026 In this note 01 · From the model to the work system 02 · Health as a property of deployment From the model to the work system AI safety commonly studies model reliability, robustness, control and misuse. At work, these properties describe only part of the risk. AI enters an organisation shaped by targets, deadlines, power asymmetries and rules of responsibility. The system actually deployed is the interaction between model, task, workers, organisation and implementation choices. Its safety cannot be inferred from a technical benchmark. Health as a property of deployment Cognitive load, intensification, surveillance, autonomy, prevented quality and employment insecurity can change without a spectacular incident. Yet these gradual effects are safety failures when they durably damage health or the capacity to act. Extending AI safety to occupational health means observing these transformations before deployment and then in real activity, with workers and prevention professionals. 03 Why it matters Safety at work is not limited to what a model does; it also depends on what its deployment does to workers. 04 Source IA Santé Travail — programme de recherche Open the source ↗ 05 Related notes 01 · NOTE When AI expands the job before it replaces it → 02 · FIELD NOTE What happens when the manager is an AI? → 03 · POLICY NOTE Compliance does not make AI deployment safe →","url":"en/research/occupational-health-ai-safety/"},{"title":"Who is responsible for safe AI deployment at work? — Research","description":"Providers, management, business teams, IT, worker representatives and prevention specialists each see part of the system. Safety depends on explicit coordination.","keywords":"← All research POLICY NOTE · 07 Governance & Prevention · AI Safety 10 AUG 2026 Who is responsible for safe AI deployment at work? Providers, management, business teams, IT, worker representatives and prevention specialists each see part of the system. Safety depends on explicit coordination. Author Dr Charles Broutin Published 10 AUG 2026 In this note 01 · Responsibility cannot be delegated to the model 02 · Organise decision loops Responsibility cannot be delegated to the model The provider knows the model; the employer organises work; users see discrepancies in activity; representatives and prevention professionals observe collective and health effects. No actor holds all the information alone. Responsibility becomes fragile when everyone assumes someone else checked safety, or when the final decision is attributed to a technical recommendation. Organise decision loops Safe deployment names the owner of each risk, defines stop criteria and makes incidents visible. It distinguishes who proposes, authorises, uses and can suspend. Governance is not a one-off meeting. It is a loop connecting design, decision, use, reporting and correction. 03 Why it matters Distributed responsibility without coordination can easily become an absence of operational accountability. 04 Source IA Santé Travail — cadre de gouvernance Open the source ↗ 05 Related notes 01 · POLICY NOTE Compliance does not make AI deployment safe → 02 · RESEARCH Occupational health is an AI safety issue → 03 · POLICY NOTE France is beginning to treat AI as an occupational-health issue →","url":"en/research/responsibility-for-safe-ai-deployment/"},{"title":"AI model landscape","description":"A dated guide to AI model families, benchmarks and selection criteria for specific workplace tasks, data and conditions of use.","keywords":"Models · Benchmarks · Use selection A useful landscape, not a podium. Rankings age quickly. Model selection should begin with the task, unacceptable errors, data, required control and the total cost of use. Compare usefully ↓ Read the benchmarks Dated view No universal best model Local testing required What supports the whole Thomas Cole The Titan’s Goblet · 1833 This world seems to rest entirely on a monumental support, yet its equilibrium depends on what carries it. The work reminds us that a model is never a sufficient foundation: reliable use also depends on data, tools, oversight and work organisation. DATE A ranking is a snapshot. Model version, harness, tools and compute budget must accompany every result. TASK The best model is contextual. Strong general performance does not guarantee quality in occupational health or French law. CYCLE An update requires re-testing. A new version, prompt, corpus or tool means the earlier result is no longer enough. 01 / Three practical families Begin with the system architecture. The commercial name matters less than how the system is hosted, connected to data and authorised to act. 01 · HOSTED Proprietary general-purpose model. Simple access, broad capability and integrated tools, balanced against provider dependence, forced version changes and contractual conditions. Examine: data, retention, location, subprocessors, stability and reversibility. 02 · OPEN WEIGHTS Model operated under local control. Greater control over hosting and updates, while the organisation takes on infrastructure, security, evaluation and maintenance. Examine: licence, internal expertise, operating cost, patches and monitoring. 03 · COMPOSED Specialised system, RAG or agent. The model is connected to a corpus, rules or tools. Performance then depends as much on this chain as on the base model. Examine: sources, permissions, logs, tool errors and stopping conditions. 02 / Selection criteria Six questions before the headline score. A useful comparison tests the exact system under the actual intended conditions. 01 Exact task. Summarise, extract, draft, classify, search or act: requirements and critical errors differ. 02 Unacceptable errors. Omissions, false sources, discrimination, disclosure and unauthorised action require separate tests. 03 Data. Information type, legal basis, retention, reuse, transfers and access control. 04 Control. Pinned version, override, traceability, tool permissions, human review and immediate stop. 05 Total cost. Service price, integration, latency, human checking, incidents, maintenance and skill retention. 06 ","url":"en/resources/models/"},{"title":"AI Psychosocial Risks at Work | Prevention Framework","description":"Identify six Gollac psychosocial-risk factors and three AI-specific mechanisms, their organisational causes and possible occupational-health effects.","keywords":"Psychosocial risks · Organisation · Health AI becomes a risk when work organisation creates exposure. A model alone does not explain a health effect. Risk develops when the use of AI changes workload, autonomy, relationships, work quality or professional security without adequate resources for regulation and control. See the nine factors ↓ Assess a project Collective analysis Real work No individual diagnosis When the balance breaks Thomas Cole Destruction · 1836 The city’s apparent order collapses when its supports can no longer hold. The work reminds us that, at work, an equilibrium can quickly become fragile when workload, accountability, routes for challenge or skills are not followed with care. 06 + 03 Nine angles of analysis. Six Gollac families, supplemented by three exploratory axes specific to AI use. CAUSE Examine the organisation. A score, error or recommendation becomes hazardous only within a particular work situation. HEALTH Think in terms of exposure. Duration, frequency, combined constraints, available resources and room for action shape the level of risk. 01 / What creates exposure Four organisational choices to examine first. Start not with “is AI dangerous?”, but with who does what, under which targets, with what authority given to the system and what real means to regain control. 01 Targets raised too quickly. Theoretical time savings become higher volumes or shorter deadlines before the actual time required for checking is known. May drive: intensity, interruptions, work spilling beyond hours. 02 Decisions removed from real work. The tool is selected without involving users, worker representatives or those who will handle errors and difficult cases. May drive: loss of autonomy, conflict, workarounds. 03 Responsibility without resources. Workers must approve outputs without enough time, sources or authority to challenge them. May drive: cognitive overload, anxiety, impossible accountability. 04 Unclear professional change. Tasks are removed or recombined without training pathways, skill maintenance or credible information about jobs. May drive: insecurity, disengagement, skill erosion. 02 / Gollac framework The six families of psychosocial risk factors. These factors describe employment and organisational conditions that may affect health. They are neither symptoms nor diagnoses. G01 Work intensity and time. Volume, deadlines, interruptions, hours and complexity. With AI: raised targets, invisible correction work, permanent alerts. G02 Emotional demands. Managing one’s emotions, distress and conflict. With AI: announcing, explaining o","url":"en/risks-prevention/"},{"title":"Understanding AI at Work","description":"A clear guide to workplace AI systems, large language models, their capabilities and limits, and how to interpret current AI benchmarks.","keywords":"Understand · Essential concepts Understand artificial intelligence at work. Distinguish between systems, understand how a language model produces an answer, and read performance results without making them say more than they do. Start reading ↓ Choose a resource 12-minute read Selected sources Updated August 2026 What seems stable Thomas Cole The Savage State · 1834 The landscape still seems open, while the mountain gives the impression of a stable landmark. The work reminds us that an apparent equilibrium can quickly become fragile when the setting changes: understanding an AI system means identifying what must remain under control — the task, the data and the decisions — before novelty hides the weak points. View the artwork ↗ 01 “AI” does not describe a single tool. Generating text, assigning a score and allocating tasks are three different functions, with different risks. 02 An LLM produces a plausible continuation. It calculates probabilities from context; it does not independently verify whether its answer is true or compliant. 03 Real work is the right level of analysis. The model matters, but so do the task, the data, the people, the decision rules and the organisation. 01 / Name the system First, what kind of artificial intelligence are we talking about? The term “AI” covers systems that do not produce the same kind of output. To understand a professional use, begin by identifying what the tool actually does. 01 · Generate Generative AI It creates or transforms text, images, sound or code from an instruction. An LLM-based conversational assistant belongs to this family. Output New content Watch for Accuracy, sources, input data 02 · Predict Predictive AI It estimates a probability, assigns a score or classifies a case using past data: default risk, case priority or diagnostic support. Output A score or category Watch for Bias, thresholds, false positives and false negatives 03 · Organise Algorithmic management It allocates tasks, sets processing priorities, measures activity or influences a management decision. It may or may not include generative AI. Output An instruction or decision Watch for Autonomy, appeal routes, work intensification 02 / Inside an LLM How a language model produces an answer. A large language model — or LLM — is a neural network trained to predict what comes next in a text. It does not retrieve a ready-made answer from a database: it composes one, unit by unit. 01 · Tokenise Text becomes tokens The prompt and supplied documents are split into small units: words, word fragments or symbols. 02 · Represent Tokens become numbe","url":"en/understand/"},{"title":"Supporting Workplace AI Deployment","description":"Assess a workplace AI project before deployment, organise a reversible pilot and monitor its effects on real work, psychosocial risks and occupational health.","keywords":"Field use · Deployment support Deploy AI without discovering its effects after the fact. Define the use before implementation, organise a reversible pilot, then compare promises with effects actually observed in the work. Choose the right questionnaire ↓ Get support 18 questions · about 10 min Data stored locally Archivable report An equilibrium still open Thomas Cole The Arcadian or Pastoral State · 1834 The landscape seems peaceful and already organised, yet its equilibrium is still being built. The work reminds us that, before deployment, the choices still open — need, participation, limits, follow-up and stopping criteria — determine the strength of what follows. View the artwork ↗ 01 · Before Turn an intention into testable hypotheses. Describe the task, the people concerned, possible errors, review workload and responsibilities. 02 · Pilot Test on a small scale, observe the real work and remain able to reverse course. A useful pilot has a defined scope, a baseline, success criteria and reasons for stopping. 03 · After Compare promises with the work actually performed. Measure final quality, time saved or shifted, autonomy, skills, team dynamics and warning signals. 01 / Two moments, two tools Document the decision beforehand. Verify the effects afterwards. The two questionnaires answer different questions. The first identifies what the project may change; the second examines what actually changed once the tool was in use. Before deployment 18 questions · ≈ 10 min Assess the project’s potential impact. Use it when defining the project, selecting a solution or preparing a pilot. The questionnaire combines the six psychosocial risk factors in the Gollac framework with three AI-specific mechanisms. Output 01 Nine-dimension profile Output 02 Priority issues Output 03 Exportable action plan Start the assessment → After deployment Dated follow-up Observe the effects in real work. Use it during a pilot or after go-live. Follow-up compares the initial objectives with the experience of people who use, review or are affected by the system’s outputs. Observe Quality and errors Compare Workload and autonomy Decide Continue, correct or stop Organise follow-up → Useful continuity Keeping the same scope, populations and a small set of common indicators makes it possible to compare the baseline, the pilot and established use. 02 / Before deployment Six decisions to make before launching a pilot. The questionnaire does not replace collective decision-making. It makes explicit the choices too often buried in a vendor presentation or productivity promise. Decision Que","url":"en/uses-and-field/"},{"title":"Workplace AI After Deployment | Monitoring Real Work","description":"Monitor the real effects of workplace AI after deployment: quality, verification workload, autonomy, skills, collective work and reversibility.","keywords":"Thomas Cole, River in the Catskills, 1843 Home › Uses & field evidence › After deployment What AI deployment produces in real work Observe quality, verification workload, jobs, skills and collective work once the system meets everyday organisational reality. In 30 seconds Essential points A successful demonstration does not guarantee integration into work or a durable benefit. Time saved may be replaced by checking, correction, escalation and a concentration of complex cases. Post-deployment monitoring must allow the organisation to redesign the system or reverse course. Audience Occupational-health teams, employers, HR, worker representatives and leaders of deployed AI systems Reading time About 7 minutes Last reviewed 19 July 2026 On this page Four field lessons Documented trajectories Monitoring indicators Beyond the demonstration Four lessons from real-world deployment The value of a system becomes visible in workflows, trade-offs, rework, skills and effects observed over time. Technical performance is not enough A system may pass a test while failing to fit everyday procedures, data quality and operational constraints. Efficiency may move the workload Faster production may generate additional checking, correction and a concentration of the most complex situations. Jobs and responsibilities change Some tasks shrink, others appear, and verification or error management becomes a new responsibility. Reversal must remain possible The organisation should be able to restore human intervention or suspend the system if quality or working conditions deteriorate. Documented trajectories Cases that must be read in context These examples illustrate possible mechanisms. They are not general proof that artificial intelligence succeeds or fails in every organisation. Onclusive: automation, employment and worker consultation In 2023, a proposed restructuring concerned 217 of 383 positions in a context of increased automation. The first procedure was later withdrawn following observations from the French administration. Occupational-health perspective: employment, work organisation, skills and working conditions must be assessed without reducing the analysis to the single label “AI”. Source: written question no. 12062, French National Assembly. Klarna: automated volume and the return of human service The company presented its conversational assistant as handling a workload equivalent to approximately 700 full-time agents. It later reinforced human involvement to improve service quality. Occupational-health perspective: volume alone is insufficient. Quality, escalatio","url":"en/uses-and-field/after-deployment/"},{"title":"Workplace AI Before Deployment | Risk and Pilot Framework","description":"A practical framework for workplace AI before deployment: select a bounded task, classify the level of vigilance and run a limited, verifiable and reversible pilot.","keywords":"Home › Uses & field evidence › Before deployment Prepare a workplace AI use before deployment Start from a real need, match safeguards to the level of risk and organise a limited pilot before any decision to scale. In 30 seconds Essential points A useful pilot begins with a bounded, measurable and easily verifiable task. Vigilance increases with sensitive data, system autonomy and the consequences of error. Human oversight, stop criteria and post-deployment indicators must be defined before the first test. Audience Employers, SMEs, occupational-health teams, HR, worker representatives and AI project leaders Reading time About 7 minutes Last reviewed 19 July 2026 On this page Should this use be tested? Vigilance levels Run a pilot Minimum pilot record Decision filter Does this AI use deserve a pilot? Technology is not a need in itself. These questions help determine whether the proposed pilot addresses an identifiable work problem. Which problem are we trying to solve? Describe an observable work difficulty without starting from a tool that has already been selected. Which task will be assisted? Identify a precise activity, its steps, the people involved and the expected result. Which data will be used? Review their sensitivity, origin, hosting, access rights and possible reuse. Who will verify the output? Name a person with the time, competence, information and authority required. How will usefulness be measured? Track quality, error, time, verification workload and effects on real work. How can the pilot be stopped? Define fallback arrangements, stop criteria and the reversibility of the process. Three levels of vigilance Match safeguards to the real workplace use The level depends on the task, data, system autonomy and people affected — not only on the model or product name. Level 1 · Lower risk Document assistance Reading, summarising, classifying or reformulating non-sensitive content without a direct effect on an individual decision. verify sources; exclude sensitive data from uncontrolled tools; review before reuse. View examples evidence scanning; reformulating training material; structuring a bibliography; preparing a document outline. Level 2 · Supervised professional use Support for professional quality Producing an alert, proposal or structure that must be reviewed and discussed by a qualified professional. anonymised cases; human reference standard; documented errors; final professional validation. View examples second reading of recommendations; drafting support for risk-assessment documents; training vignettes; structuring field observation","url":"en/uses-and-field/before-deployment/"},{"title":"AI in Occupational Health Services and France’s PST 5","description":"What France’s Occupational Health Plan 2026–2030 says about AI at work, practical AI uses in occupational health services and results from INRS study TF 335.","keywords":"OHS services · Professional uses · Human oversight AI can support an OHS service when the task is precise. Reviewing a document, finding a source, structuring observations or preparing training: value does not come from “AI” in general, but from a limited use that can be tested and corrected. An evaluated example. A study published by INRS in June 2026 tested a language model as a second reader of occupational physician recommendations. It shows genuine potential, but not an ability to replace the occupational physician. See the study and results ↓ Explore the uses Limited task Human reference Decision retained Between two sides Thomas Cole The Oxbow · 1836 Between the storm and the valley, the landscape rests in an uncertain balance. The work reminds us that every promising use must be considered from both sides: what it improves and what it may make fragile if its effects are not observed. View the painting at The Met ↗ PST 5 · 2026–2030 France’s national plan makes AI at work an occupational health issue. Under Objective 3, France’s Occupational Health Plan 2026–2030 states that AI-related transformations should be introduced through consultation, protect working conditions and be assessed through their real effects. Sub-action 3.1.1 provides for a national Observatory for AI at Work, led by Anact. 01 Investigate both sides. PST 5 calls for evidence on AI as a tool for preventing occupational risks and improving working conditions, while also examining AI systems and their uses as possible sources of occupational risk. 02 Produce evidence that supports action. The plan provides for quantitative and qualitative studies, experiments, training, webinars, and practical tools and methods that can turn findings into prevention measures. 03 Give OHS services a defined role. Annex 3 calls them “pivotal actors”. It asks them to contribute evidence on AI with Présanse and Anact, while adopting a measured use of AI in their own activities. For an inter-company occupational health service (SPSTI), the task is therefore twofold: test clearly bounded professional uses with care, and observe what AI deployment changes in client organisations — workload, autonomy, skills, teams and occupational risks. Source: France’s Occupational Health Plan 2026–2030, Action 3.1.1, pp. 44–45, and Annex 3, pp. 154–156. Read the official document (French) ↗ 01 / Where AI may help Six concrete uses with clearly defined human responsibility. Except for the review case presented below, these uses are possibilities to experiment with and assess in each service. 01 · Occupational medicine","url":"en/uses-and-field/occupational-health-example/"}];
